1 OCCU assessor, reseinspektör och dispaschör hos Ostindiska Kompaniet i Göteborg
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.nad.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Presentation.aspx?id=18029
2 CONT
2 CONT "Okänd ostindieresenär med proppfull skrivbordslåda Christopher Hinric Braad (1728–1781) är den relativt okände skeppskrivaren vid Ostindiska kompaniet som gjorde karriär genom att lämna in ett par utsökt genomarbetade reseskildri
2 CONC ngar till kompaniets ledning efter sina resor till Kina och Indien vid mitten av 1700-talet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Inga av Braads texter har någonsin tryckts, men delar av dem finns nu att läsa i en ny avhandling i nordiska språk från Göteborgs universitet." .
2 CONT
2 CONT Rickard Melkersson: Skrifterna från Hoppet. C.H. Braads ostindiska resa 1748–49 Institutionen för svenska språket, Göteborgs universitet Disputation: 7 juni 2013 Fakultetsopponent: professor Jonas Carlqvist, Institutionen för språ
2 CONC kstudier, Umeå universitet Publicerad den 18 augusti 2013, Nättidningen Svensk Historia
2 CONT
2 CONT https://journals.openedition.org/afriques/4910
2 CONT ”Born in Stockholm in 1728, Braad lived in his mother Gertrude’s home town of Torneå, northern Sweden, where his father Poul was an official, moving to Norrköping with his family at age six. After private tutoring and learning sev
2 CONC eral languages, he went to Uppsala University aged 14, but left dissatisfied. Briefly working as a clerk, he joined the SOIC, sailing for the first time in January 1748 as ship’s clerk on the Hoppet [Hope], on a voyage to Canton. 
2 CONC He made further journeys in 1750–52 (on the Götha Leijon), then again in 1753, when, having reached Canton, he was directed to travel independently to gather natural history material in Southeast Asia and India, much of which time
2 CONC  he spent in Bengal, considered a spy by the British but cleverly diverting their attention. He lost almost all his papers from 1754–58, some earlier ones, and “two chests with many rare natural-history specimens of all the realms
2 CONC  of nature [in] Asia” in a shipwreck on his return voyage to Europe in 1758. He got back to Sweden in summer 1759, writing his account of Malaya and Bengal that autumn. He met Carl Linné (Linnaeus) to discuss his findings in 1760.
2 CONC  On his first two journeys the ship’s pastor was Olof Torén, a pupil and correspondent of Linné, and there is evidence in Torén’s correspondence with Linné of discussions with Braad, although he is not mentioned. Torén died in 175
2 CONC 3, a depressive and alcoholic according to Braad’s unpublished autobiography. Promoted to supercargo, Braad sailed again for Surat in 1760, but by then the British were foiling all other traders; the mission was not a commercial s
2 CONC uccess there, but did better in China. Back home in 1763, he was offered, but declined, a directorship in the SOIC, preferring to retire at age 35 with the money he had made in permitted private trading. He settled back in Norrköp
2 CONC ing, marrying and being widowed twice, then marrying a third wife in 1772. He had three daughters by his first wife, and a son by his third. He built up an impressive library of 3,000 books. Apparently still in good health, he beg
2 CONC an a more detailed account of his life but died in October 1781, just four months after starting it.” 
2 CONT A Swedish traveller in the Comoro Islands: The description of Anjouan/Nzwani by Christopher Henric Braad in 1750, translated and annotated
2 CONT Un voyageur suédois aux îles Comores : la description d’Anjouan/Nzwani par Christophe Henric Braad en 1750, traduite en anglais et commentée
2 CONT Jeremy Franks† et Anthony S. Cheke
